PUBLIC RESPONSE: I’m with Colleagues 1-3,5-6-7 on the 3日/三日 reading.

The four clauses are a component-poetic, row-wise walk through the stack 日日日 = 晶 jīng.

* 一横、又一横、再一横 — the three top bars of the three 日, the opening 3H block;
* 一竖、又一竖、再一竖 — the three left pillars of the three 日 laid down in order;
* 一竖在左、一竖在右 — the box-form left–right framing of a 日, quoted here as the pivot between the left pillars and the right pillars of the stack;
* 一竖、又一竖、再一竖 — the three right pillars of the three 日, the closing 3V block.

That is the familiar 3H + 3V + 2V + 3V poetic grouping for the literal 6H + 6V = 12-stroke triplication of 日. The bottom horizontals are omitted in the poetic summary, as is normal for the riddle.

品 pǐn is the proximate mnemonic confusion: it is taught as 三横两竖，一竖在左一竖在右 and is the skeletal three-strata form underlying the same visual, but it cannot give the two distinct triple-vertical blocks 3V before the left/right clause and 3V after it; it has one left pillar and one right pillar.

非 fails on ordering: it would need the horizontals interleaved with the verticals and a single global left-right demarcation, not an initial 3H → 3V → left/right pair → 3V sequence.

The exact sequencing of three initial horizontals, a first triple of verticals, an explicit left/right framing clause, and a second triple of verticals is the classic school-book description of 晶, the three suns.

